**HTML5 game performance optimization** focuses on keeping frame rates high (ideally 60 FPS), reducing jank, lowering battery use, and ensuring smooth play on both desktop and mobile.

### 1. Rendering Pipeline
- Prefer a single `<canvas>` (or WebGL) over many DOM elements. Canvas is far faster for games.
- Always drive the main loop with `requestAnimationFrame` instead of `setInterval`/`setTimeout`.
- Only redraw what changed (dirty rectangles or layered canvases for static backgrounds).
- Batch draw calls: use sprite sheets / texture atlases so you minimize state changes.
- For 2D: stick to `CanvasRenderingContext2D` unless you need heavy effects — then use WebGL (via libraries like PixiJS or Three.js).
- Avoid `getImageData` / `putImageData` every frame; they are very expensive.

```js
function gameLoop(timestamp) {
  const dt = timestamp - lastTime;
  lastTime = timestamp;
  update(dt);
  render();
  requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
}
requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
```

### 2. JavaScript & Game Logic
- Keep the update loop lightweight. Move heavy work (pathfinding, AI, physics) to Web Workers when possible.
- Use object pooling for frequently created/destroyed objects (bullets, particles, enemies) to avoid garbage-collection spikes.
- Prefer typed arrays (`Float32Array`, `Uint8Array`) for large datasets.
- Minimize allocations inside the hot loop (no new objects, arrays, or closures every frame).
- Use spatial partitioning (quadtrees, grids) for collision detection instead of O(n²) checks.

### 3. Assets & Loading
- Compress images (WebP or optimized PNG). Use sprite sheets.
- Load assets progressively or on demand; show a loading screen.
- Decode audio with the Web Audio API and reuse `AudioBuffer`s.
- Prefer power-of-two textures if using WebGL.
- Cache assets in IndexedDB or the Cache API for offline/replay scenarios.

### 4. Mobile & Cross-Device Tips
- Scale the canvas to the device pixel ratio carefully (`window.devicePixelRatio`) but clamp it on low-end devices to avoid overdraw.
- Use touch events efficiently; avoid passive listeners only when you need `preventDefault`.
- Detect low-end hardware and lower particle counts, resolution, or effects.
- Pause the game (and audio) when the tab is hidden (`visibilitychange` event).
- Test on real devices — Chrome DevTools remote debugging is essential.

### 5. Profiling & Measurement
- Chrome DevTools → Performance tab: record a session, look for long frames, GC pauses, and paint times.
- Use `performance.now()` or the built-in FPS counter.
- Watch for forced synchronous layout / reflow (avoid reading layout properties after writes).
- Lighthouse or WebPageTest can give high-level scores, but game-specific profiling is more useful.

### 6. Common Libraries That Help
- **Rendering**: PixiJS (excellent 2D WebGL), Phaser, Babylon.js, Three.js.
- **Physics**: Matter.js, Planck.js, or Cannon.js (keep bodies simple).
- **Audio**: Howler.js or native Web Audio.
- These libraries already implement many of the optimizations above.

### Quick Wins Checklist
| Area              | Action                                      | Impact     |
|-------------------|---------------------------------------------|------------|
| Loop              | `requestAnimationFrame`                     | High       |
| Objects           | Object pooling                              | High       |
| Drawing           | Sprite sheets + batching                    | High       |
| Collisions        | Spatial hash / quadtree                     | High       |
| Assets            | WebP + compression                          | Medium     |
| GC                | Minimize allocations in hot path            | High       |
| Mobile            | Cap DPR + reduce particles on low-end       | Medium-High|

Start by measuring (DevTools Performance), fix the biggest bottlenecks first, then iterate. Most HTML5 games become smooth once the render and update loops stop doing unnecessary work every frame.

